# Without this, `npm install` in this directory FAILS OUTRIGHT — so a fresh
# clone cannot build the app at all.
#
# The cause is a stale peer range, not a real incompatibility.
# `lucide-react-native@0.468.0` declares:
#
#     "peerDependencies": { "react": "^16.5.1 || ^17.0.0 || ^18.0.0" }
#
# Expo SDK 54 pins react 19.1.0. Lucide simply has not published a range that
# admits React 19; the package itself is a set of SVG icons rendered through
# react-native-svg and touches none of the APIs that changed. The same applies
# to react-dom, which @expo/metro-runtime pulls in for the web target.
#
# So the peer graph is wrong and the code is fine, which is exactly the case
# this flag exists for.
#
# Two things to know:
#
#   This file is scoped to apps/mobile ON PURPOSE. Do NOT add it at the repo
#   root. Installing the workspaces with legacy-peer-deps plants a nested
#   @types/react that ignores the root override and silently breaks the admin
#   console's r3f JSX types — that has already happened once, and recovering
#   from it needs a full node_modules and lockfile wipe. See the README.
#
#   Revisit when lucide-react-native publishes a React 19 peer range: drop this
#   file, run `npm install`, and confirm it resolves cleanly.
legacy-peer-deps=true
